# Desktop/dev apt packages for graphical Linux machines (display = true).
# Installed by run_after_install-packages.sh (stamp-and-compare, like the Pi).
# Distinct from bootstrap.apt: those are minimal pre-Homebrew prereqs on EVERY
# Linux machine; these are packages only a machine with a display needs —
# headless servers and Coder workspaces never get them.
#
# The vendor-repo apps below need their apt repos + keys, which are provisioned
# first by run_onchange_before_15-linux-desktop-repos.sh (runs in the *before*
# group). GUI apps available on Flathub come from the Brewfile's `flatpak`
# entries instead; only apps whose best Linux channel is a vendor apt repo (or
# Ubuntu's own archive, like ghostty) live here.

# System libraries — belong in apt, not the Brewfile: a Homebrew libX11 under
# /home/linuxbrew would shadow the system one the running X server uses.
# X11 development headers for native crates that link libX11 via pkg-config
# (rdev: Xlib/XRecord/XInput; enigo: xkbcommon).
libx11-dev
libxtst-dev
libxi-dev
libxkbcommon-dev
libxkbcommon-x11-dev

# Vendor-repo desktop apps (repos set up by the before-hook above):
#   google-chrome-stable  -> dl.google.com
#   signal-desktop        -> updates.signal.org
#   code (VS Code)        -> packages.microsoft.com
#   warp-terminal         -> releases.warp.dev
#   tailscale             -> pkgs.tailscale.com
#   ghostty               -> Ubuntu 26.04+ official archive (no vendor repo)
#   flatpak               -> runtime only; Flathub GUI apps come via the Brewfile
# NOTE: names only, one per line — the installer extracts via `grep -Ev '^#|^$'`,
# which does not strip inline comments.
google-chrome-stable
signal-desktop
code
warp-terminal
tailscale
ghostty
flatpak
